---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import FurtherReading from '../../components/FurtherReading.astro';
import SeaBreak from '../../components/SeaBreak.astro';
import { celestial, scenes } from '../../config/images';
import Breadcrumbs from '../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import CTABand from '../../components/CTABand.astro';
const crumbs = [
  { label: 'Explore Astrology', href: '/explore/' },
  { label: 'Relationship Astrology', href: '/explore/relationships/' },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Relationship Astrology, synastry and composite charts"
  description="How relationship astrology works: synastry compares two natal charts, composite techniques describe the relationship as a whole, and neither reduces compatibility to a score."
  path="/explore/relationships/"
  breadcrumbs={crumbs}
>
  <Breadcrumbs crumbs={crumbs} />
  <section class="hero hero--split">
    <div class="container">
      <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">Explore Astrology</p>
      <h1>Relationship astrology</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        A relationship is not one chart added to another. It is two whole people meeting, each
        bringing a different set of needs, habits, strengths, sensitivities, and choices. The
        astrology becomes interesting in the interaction.
      </p>
      </div>
      <img class="hero__img" src={scenes.relationship.src} srcset={scenes.relationship.srcset} sizes="100vw" alt={scenes.relationship.alt} width={scenes.relationship.width} height={scenes.relationship.height} fetchpriority="high" />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container prose flow">
      <h2>Synastry: how two charts meet</h2>
      <p>
        Synastry compares the placements in one natal chart with those in another. The aspects
        between the two charts can describe attraction, familiarity, friction, support, challenge,
        communication patterns, differing needs, and the places where one person reliably activates
        something in the other.
      </p>
      <p>
        The point is not to reduce those contacts to “good” or “bad.” A supportive aspect can become
        complacent; a difficult one can become productive; either can be handled well or poorly. I
        want to know how the people involved are actually living the dynamic.
      </p>
      <h2>The composite: looking at the relationship as a whole</h2>
      <p>
        A composite chart is calculated from the midpoints between two natal charts and is used by
        many astrologers as a symbolic chart of the relationship itself. It adds a different question:
        not only “What happens between these two people?” but “What kind of pattern seems to emerge
        when these two lives are joined?”
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <SeaBreak image={celestial.clearedTable} />

  <section class="section section--sea">
    <div class="container prose flow">
      <h2>Compatibility is not a verdict</h2>
      <p>
        I do not believe there is a chart combination that guarantees success or failure. What the
        astrology can offer is a vocabulary for the relationship: where understanding comes easily,
        where the same misunderstanding may repeat, what each person experiences as care, what each
        person protects, and where expectations may not match.
      </p>
      <p>
        That can be valuable because naming a pattern gives two people something more precise to talk
        about. It still does not make the decision for them. A chart can describe the terrain; the
        relationship is lived by the people in it.
      </p>

      <h2>Beyond romance</h2>
      <p>
        These techniques can be useful for many important one-to-one relationships: partners,
        parents and adult children, close friends, business partners, or others whose lives are
        meaningfully intertwined. The questions change with the relationship, but the principle is
        the same: two individual charts, one relationship, and a great deal of context that no
        compatibility score can capture.
      </p>
      <p>
        That's the territory of the <a href="/readings/relationship/">Relationship Consultation</a>.
        Both people's birth data is needed, and the work is strongest when both people can be part
        of the conversation.
      </p>
      <FurtherReading />
    </div>
  </section>

  <CTABand
    heading="Two charts, one relationship"
    body="The useful question is not whether the charts are compatible. It is how these two people meet, where the patterns repeat, and what they want to do with that understanding."
    buttonLabel="About the Relationship Consultation"
    buttonHref="/readings/relationship/"
  />
</BaseLayout>
